Day 289/300: Find All Possible Stable Binary Arrays II

Problem Statement #3130

Problem Statement Expplantion:

You are given three integers zero, one, and limit. 
A binary array is called stable if:
The number of 0's in the array is exactly zero.
The number of 1's in the array is exactly one.
Each subarray of length greater than limit contains both 0 and 1. (In other words, no more than limit consecutive 0s or 1s).
Return the total number of stable binary arrays modulo 10^9+7.

Solution Approach:

The "Hard" version of this problem requires an O(zero×one) approach. A naive DP that counts consecutive elements would lead to O(zero×one×limit), which is too slow.

DP State
We define dp[i][j][k] as the number of stable arrays with i zeros and j ones, where:

k = 0: The last element added was a 0.
k = 1: The last element added was a 1.
Transitions & Inclusion-Exclusion
To calculate dp[i][j][0]:

Add a 0: We can potentially add a 0 to any valid array of size (i−1,j).

dp[i][j][0]=dp[i−1][j][0]+dp[i−1][j][1]
Handle the Limit: The above sum includes cases where we might have just created a sequence of limit + 1 zeros.
Subtract Invalid Cases: A sequence becomes invalid if it already had limit zeros and we added one more. This only happens if, at the state (i−limit−1,j), the last bit placed was a 1.
dp[i][j][0]=(dp[i−1][j][0]+dp[i−1][j][1]−dp[i−limit−1][j][1])
The same logic applies symmetrically to dp[i][j][1] by tracking the limit for ones.

Here's the Code:

class Solution {
    public int numberOfStableArrays(int zero, int one, int limit) {
        int MOD = 1_000_000_007;
        long[][][] dp = new long[zero + 1][one + 1][2];

        for (int i = 0; i <= Math.min(zero, limit); i++) dp[i][0][0] = 1;
        for (int j = 0; j <= Math.min(one, limit); j++) dp[0][j][1] = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i <= zero; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= one; j++) {
                dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i - 1][j][0] + dp[i - 1][j][1]) % MOD;
                if (i > limit) {
                    dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] - dp[i - limit - 1][j][1] + MOD) % MOD;
                }

                dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j - 1][0] + dp[i][j - 1][1]) % MOD;
                if (j > limit) {
                    dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] - dp[i][j - limit - 1][0] + MOD) % MOD;
                }
            }
        }

        return (int) ((dp[zero][one][0] + dp[zero][one][1]) % MOD);
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(1)
Space Complexity: O(1)




